Our login screen supports passwords, Google and magic links. Every new provider has to implement this interface, and half the methods end up throwing. Give me a SOLID critique and a better shape:

```typescript
export interface AuthProvider {
  loginWithPassword(username: string, password: string): Promise<Session>;
  startOAuth(redirectUri: string): string;
  completeOAuth(code: string): Promise<Session>;
  sendMagicLink(address: string): Promise<void>;
  consumeMagicLink(token: string): Promise<Session>;
  changePassword(userId: string, oldPw: string, newPw: string): Promise<void>;
  refresh(session: Session): Promise<Session>;
}

export class GoogleAuthProvider implements AuthProvider {
  loginWithPassword(): Promise<Session> { throw new Error('Not supported'); }
  startOAuth(redirectUri: string) { return `https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/auth?redirect_uri=${redirectUri}`; }
  async completeOAuth(code: string) { /* exchange code */ return this.toSession(code); }
  sendMagicLink(): Promise<void> { throw new Error('Not supported'); }
  consumeMagicLink(): Promise<Session> { throw new Error('Not supported'); }
  changePassword(): Promise<void> { throw new Error('Not supported'); }
  async refresh(session: Session) { /* refresh token */ return session; }
  private toSession(code: string): Session { return { userId: code, expiresAt: 0 }; }
}

// login.ts
for (const p of providers) {
  if (p.constructor.name !== 'GoogleAuthProvider') buttons.push(passwordForm(p));
}
```

### SOLID Critique

#### Interface Segregation Principle — the central violation
`AuthProvider` bundles four unrelated capabilities: password login, the OAuth redirect flow, magic links and password management. `GoogleAuthProvider` needs two of them (plus refresh) and is forced to "implement" four methods by throwing. Every new provider pays the same tax, and every change to one capability (say, `sendMagicLink` gains a `locale` parameter) forces edits in classes that never supported it.

#### Liskov Substitution — violated as a consequence
Callers holding an `AuthProvider` reasonably call `loginWithPassword`, and Google throws `'Not supported'`. The type promises something the object cannot do, so substitutability is a lie that only fails at runtime.

#### Open/Closed — violated in `login.ts`
```text
if (p.constructor.name !== 'GoogleAuthProvider') buttons.push(passwordForm(p));
```
The UI works around the fat interface by checking a class name string. Add GitHub OAuth and you must edit this condition; minify the bundle and `constructor.name` becomes `"t"`, so the password form appears for Google.

#### Not a violation: Single Responsibility of `GoogleAuthProvider`
Once the stubs are removed, the class has one reason to change: Google's OAuth protocol. Its responsibility is fine; the interface it is forced into is the problem.

---

### Refactoring

Give each capability its own interface. A provider implements only what it supports, and the UI discovers capabilities through types, not class names.

```typescript
export interface Session {
  readonly userId: string;
  readonly expiresAt: Date;
}

export type AuthError =
  | { kind: 'InvalidCredentials' }
  | { kind: 'LinkExpired' }
  | { kind: 'ProviderUnavailable'; provider: string };

export interface PasswordLogin {
  readonly kind: 'password';
  login(username: string, password: string): Promise<Result<Session, AuthError>>;
}

export interface RedirectLogin {
  readonly kind: 'redirect';
  readonly label: string;
  authorizationUrl(redirectUri: string, state: string): string;
  complete(code: string): Promise<Result<Session, AuthError>>;
}

export interface MagicLinkLogin {
  readonly kind: 'magic-link';
  send(address: string): Promise<void>;
  consume(token: string): Promise<Result<Session, AuthError>>;
}

export type LoginMethod = PasswordLogin | RedirectLogin | MagicLinkLogin;

export interface SessionRefresher {
  refresh(session: Session): Promise<Result<Session, AuthError>>;
}

export interface PasswordChanger {
  change(userId: string, current: string, next: string): Promise<Result<void, AuthError>>;
}
```

Google now implements exactly what it supports, with no stubs:

```typescript
export interface GoogleTokenClient {
  exchange(code: string): Promise<{ sub: string; expiresInSeconds: number } | null>;
}

export class GoogleLogin implements RedirectLogin {
  readonly kind = 'redirect';
  readonly label = 'Continue with Google';

  constructor(private readonly client: GoogleTokenClient, private readonly clientId: string, private readonly now: () => Date) {}

  authorizationUrl(redirectUri: string, state: string): string {
    const params = new URLSearchParams({ client_id: this.clientId, redirect_uri: redirectUri, state, response_type: 'code' });
    return `https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth?${params.toString()}`;
  }

  async complete(code: string): Promise<Result<Session, AuthError>> {
    const token = await this.client.exchange(code);
    if (!token) return Result.fail({ kind: 'InvalidCredentials' });
    const expiresAt = new Date(this.now().getTime() + token.expiresInSeconds * 1000);
    return Result.ok({ userId: token.sub, expiresAt });
  }
}
```

The login screen switches on the `kind` discriminant, so the compiler tells you when a new kind of method appears:

```typescript
export type LoginWidget = { type: 'form' } | { type: 'button'; label: string } | { type: 'link-request' };

export function widgetFor(method: LoginMethod): LoginWidget {
  switch (method.kind) {
    case 'password':
      return { type: 'form' };
    case 'redirect':
      return { type: 'button', label: method.label };
    case 'magic-link':
      return { type: 'link-request' };
  }
}
```

Adding GitHub is one more `RedirectLogin`; nothing in the UI changes. The redirect URL is also built with `URLSearchParams`, which fixes the unescaped `redirect_uri` in the original.

We need a use case to book a car service appointment. It must use the workshop's existing calendar interface:

```typescript
export interface BayCalendar {
  freeBays(start: Date, end: Date): Promise<string[]>;
  reserve(bayId: string, start: Date, end: Date, appointmentId: string): Promise<void>;
}
```

Rules (workshop local time): open Monday to Saturday; work starts at 08:00 or later and must finish by 17:00; start times are on the hour or half hour; bookings must be made at least 24 hours ahead. Service durations: OIL_CHANGE 60 min, TIRES 45, BRAKES 120, FULL_SERVICE 180. The appointment lasts the sum of the selected services (at least one, no duplicates). Vehicles whose model year is 10 or more years before the current year get a mandatory 30-minute safety inspection. The first free bay is assigned.

The schedule rules only need the start time, the services, the model year and "now", so they form a pure planning function. `BayCalendar` stays as it is and is injected into the use case next to a repository for the appointment itself.

#### Domain (`src/domain/servicePlan.ts`)
```typescript
import { Result } from '../shared/Result';

export type ServiceType = 'OIL_CHANGE' | 'TIRES' | 'BRAKES' | 'FULL_SERVICE';

export const DURATION_MIN: Record<ServiceType, number> = { OIL_CHANGE: 60, TIRES: 45, BRAKES: 120, FULL_SERVICE: 180 };
export const INSPECTION_MIN = 30;
export const INSPECTION_AGE_YEARS = 10;
const OPEN_MIN = 8 * 60;
const CLOSE_MIN = 17 * 60;
const SUNDAY = 0;
const MIN_LEAD_MS = 24 * 3_600_000;

export interface ServicePlan {
  readonly start: Date;
  readonly end: Date;
  readonly services: readonly ServiceType[];
  readonly inspection: boolean;
}

export type BookingError =
  | { kind: 'NoServices' }
  | { kind: 'DuplicateService'; service: ServiceType }
  | { kind: 'ClosedDay' }
  | { kind: 'InvalidStartTime' }
  | { kind: 'OutsideOpeningHours' }
  | { kind: 'TooShortNotice' }
  | { kind: 'NoBayAvailable' };

export function planService(
  services: readonly ServiceType[],
  modelYear: number,
  start: Date,
  now: Date,
): Result<ServicePlan, BookingError> {
  if (services.length === 0) return Result.fail({ kind: 'NoServices' });
  const duplicate = services.find((s, i) => services.indexOf(s) !== i);
  if (duplicate) return Result.fail({ kind: 'DuplicateService', service: duplicate });

  if (start.getDay() === SUNDAY) return Result.fail({ kind: 'ClosedDay' });
  if (start.getMinutes() % 30 !== 0 || start.getSeconds() !== 0) return Result.fail({ kind: 'InvalidStartTime' });
  if (start.getTime() - now.getTime() < MIN_LEAD_MS) return Result.fail({ kind: 'TooShortNotice' });

  const inspection = now.getFullYear() - modelYear >= INSPECTION_AGE_YEARS;
  const minutes = services.reduce((sum, s) => sum + DURATION_MIN[s], 0) + (inspection ? INSPECTION_MIN : 0);
  const startMin = start.getHours() * 60 + start.getMinutes();
  if (startMin < OPEN_MIN || startMin + minutes > CLOSE_MIN) return Result.fail({ kind: 'OutsideOpeningHours' });

  return Result.ok({ start, end: new Date(start.getTime() + minutes * 60_000), services, inspection });
}
```

#### Application (`src/application/BookServiceAppointment.ts`)
```typescript
import { Result } from '../shared/Result';
import { BookingError, ServicePlan, ServiceType, planService } from '../domain/servicePlan';

export interface Appointment extends ServicePlan {
  readonly id: string;
  readonly bayId: string;
  readonly vehicleId: string;
}

export interface AppointmentRepository {
  add(appointment: Appointment): Promise<void>;
}

export interface BookServiceInput {
  readonly vehicleId: string;
  readonly modelYear: number;
  readonly services: readonly ServiceType[];
  readonly start: Date;
}

export class BookServiceAppointment {
  constructor(
    private readonly calendar: BayCalendar,
    private readonly appointments: AppointmentRepository,
    private readonly now: () => Date,
    private readonly newId: () => string,
  ) {}

  async execute(input: BookServiceInput): Promise<Result<Appointment, BookingError>> {
    const plan = planService(input.services, input.modelYear, input.start, this.now());
    if (!plan.success) return plan;

    const [bayId] = await this.calendar.freeBays(plan.data.start, plan.data.end);
    if (bayId === undefined) return Result.fail({ kind: 'NoBayAvailable' });

    const appointment: Appointment = { ...plan.data, id: this.newId(), bayId, vehicleId: input.vehicleId };
    await this.calendar.reserve(bayId, appointment.start, appointment.end, appointment.id);
    await this.appointments.add(appointment);
    return Result.ok(appointment);
  }
}
```

Example: a 2014 car booked in 2025 for BRAKES + OIL_CHANGE needs 120 + 60 + 30 = 210 minutes, so the latest valid start is 13:30 (finishing at 17:00); 14:00 fails with `OutsideOpeningHours`. The domain uses `getDay()`/`getHours()`, which assumes the process runs in the workshop's time zone; if it does not, the composition root should pass dates already converted to workshop time.

