SOLID review please. This loads plugins for our build tool:

```typescript
import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';
import { registry } from './registry';

export class PluginLoader {
  load(dir: string): void {
    for (const file of fs.readdirSync(dir)) {
      if (!file.endsWith('.js')) continue;
      const mod = require(path.join(dir, file));
      const plugin = (mod.default ?? mod) as Plugin;
      if (!plugin.name || typeof plugin.run !== 'function') {
        console.warn(`Skipping ${file}: not a plugin`);
        continue;
      }
      if (registry.has(plugin.name)) {
        console.warn(`Duplicate plugin ${plugin.name}, keeping the first one`);
        continue;
      }
      registry.set(plugin.name, plugin);
      console.log(`Loaded ${plugin.name}`);
    }
  }
}
```

### SOLID Review

#### Open/Closed — not violated
A naive reading flags the loop, but the design is open for extension: dropping a new `.js` file into the folder adds a plugin without touching `PluginLoader`. That is exactly what OCP asks for, so keep that property.

#### Dependency Inversion — violated
The loading policy (what counts as a plugin, what happens on duplicates) depends directly on low-level details:
- `fs.readdirSync` and `require`: the rules cannot run against an in-memory list, and the loader can never support ESM plugins (`import()`), a remote catalogue or bundled built-ins without being rewritten.
- the imported module-level `registry` singleton: two loaders (e.g. one per workspace in a monorepo) write into the same global map, and tests leak state into each other.
- `console`: callers cannot collect the warnings to show them in the build summary or fail CI on them.

#### Single Responsibility — violated
One method discovers files, imports modules, validates their shape, resolves duplicates, mutates a registry and reports progress. The duplicate rule ("first one wins") is a policy decision buried between I/O calls; changing it to "fail the build" means editing file-system code.

#### Type safety (supports the above)
`as Plugin` asserts the shape before checking it, then checks it with ad hoc property reads. The validation should be a type guard on `unknown`, so the compiler and the reader agree on what a plugin is.

---

### Refactoring

```typescript
export interface Plugin {
  readonly name: string;
  run(context: BuildContext): Promise<void>;
}

export interface BuildContext {
  readonly rootDir: string;
}

export interface PluginCandidate {
  readonly source: string; // file path, URL, "builtin:..."
  readonly exported: unknown;
}

export interface PluginSource {
  candidates(): Promise<PluginCandidate[]>;
}

export type LoadIssue =
  | { kind: 'NotAPlugin'; source: string }
  | { kind: 'Duplicate'; name: string; source: string };

function isPlugin(value: unknown): value is Plugin {
  if (typeof value !== 'object' || value === null) return false;
  const name: unknown = Reflect.get(value, 'name');
  const run: unknown = Reflect.get(value, 'run');
  return typeof name === 'string' && name.length > 0 && typeof run === 'function';
}

function unwrapDefault(exported: unknown): unknown {
  if (typeof exported === 'object' && exported !== null && 'default' in exported) {
    return Reflect.get(exported, 'default');
  }
  return exported;
}

export class PluginLoader {
  constructor(private readonly sources: readonly PluginSource[]) {}

  async load(): Promise<{ plugins: Map<string, Plugin>; issues: LoadIssue[] }> {
    const plugins = new Map<string, Plugin>();
    const issues: LoadIssue[] = [];

    for (const source of this.sources) {
      for (const candidate of await source.candidates()) {
        const value = unwrapDefault(candidate.exported);
        if (!isPlugin(value)) {
          issues.push({ kind: 'NotAPlugin', source: candidate.source });
        } else if (plugins.has(value.name)) {
          issues.push({ kind: 'Duplicate', name: value.name, source: candidate.source });
        } else {
          plugins.set(value.name, value);
        }
      }
    }
    return { plugins, issues };
  }
}
```

The file-system detail becomes one `PluginSource` implementation, and it can use `import()` so ESM plugins work too:

```typescript
import { readdir } from 'node:fs/promises';
import { join } from 'node:path';
import { pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';

export class DirectoryPluginSource implements PluginSource {
  constructor(private readonly dir: string) {}

  async candidates(): Promise<PluginCandidate[]> {
    const files = (await readdir(this.dir)).filter((f) => f.endsWith('.js') || f.endsWith('.mjs'));
    return Promise.all(
      files.map(async (file) => {
        const source = join(this.dir, file);
        const exported: unknown = await import(pathToFileURL(source).href);
        return { source, exported };
      }),
    );
  }
}
```

What changed and why:
- **No global registry.** `load()` returns the map; the caller owns it. Two workspaces get two maps.
- **Issues are data.** The CLI prints them, CI can fail on `Duplicate`, and a test asserts on them, all without capturing `console`.
- **Sources are pluggable.** Built-in plugins become a `PluginSource` returning in-memory candidates, which is also what a test uses.
- **Order of sources is the duplicate policy.** Passing `[builtins, projectDir]` means built-ins win, made explicit at the composition root rather than by directory listing order.

```typescript
// Domain/ValueObjects/Money.ts

export interface Currency {
  readonly code: string;
  readonly symbol: string;
  readonly decimalPlaces: number;
}

export interface MoneyError {
  readonly type: 'InvalidAmount' | 'CurrencyMismatch' | 'InvalidCurrency';
  readonly message: string;
}

export class Money {
  private constructor(
    private readonly _amount: number,
    private readonly _currency: Currency
  ) {}

  get amount(): number {
    return this._amount;
  }

  get currency(): Currency {
    return this._currency;
  }

  /**
   * Creates a new Money instance with validation
   */
  static create(amount: number, currency: Currency): Result<Money, MoneyError> {
    if (amount < 0) {
      return Result.failure({
        type: 'InvalidAmount',
        message: 'Amount must be positive'
      });
    }

    if (!currency || !currency.code) {
      return Result.failure({
        type: 'InvalidCurrency',
        message: 'Invalid currency provided'
      });
    }

    return Result.success(new Money(amount, currency));
  }

  /**
   * Adds two Money instances with currency validation
   */
  add(other: Money): Result<Money, MoneyError> {
    if (this._currency.code !== other._currency.code) {
      return Result.failure({
        type: 'CurrencyMismatch',
        message: `Cannot add money in different currencies: ${this._currency.code} and ${other._currency.code}`
      });
    }

    const newAmount = this._amount + other._amount;
    return Result.success(new Money(newAmount, this._currency));
  }

  /**
   * Checks if two Money instances are equal
   */
  equals(other: Money): boolean {
    return (
      this._amount === other._amount &&
      this._currency.code === other._currency.code
    );
  }

  /**
   * Returns a string representation of the Money instance
   */
  toString(): string {
    return `${this._currency.symbol}${this._amount.toFixed(this._currency.decimalPlaces)}`;
  }
}

// Result type for error handling
export class Result<T, E> {
  private constructor(
    public readonly isSuccess: boolean,
    public readonly value?: T,
    public readonly error?: E
  ) {}

  static success<T, E>(value: T): Result<T, E> {
    return new Result<T, E>(true, value);
  }

  static failure<T, E>(error: E): Result<T, E> {
    return new Result<T, E>(false, undefined, error);
  }

  map<U>(fn: (value: T) => U): Result<U, E> {
    if (this.isSuccess) {
      return Result.success(fn(this.value!));
    }
    return Result.failure(this.error!);
  }

  flatMap<U>(fn: (value: T) => Result<U, E>): Result<U, E> {
    if (this.isSuccess) {
      return fn(this.value!);
    }
    return Result.failure(this.error!);
  }
}
```

Refactor the following typescript code to adhere strictly to Clean Architecture and Ports & Adapters:

```typescript
import { useState } from 'react';
import { supabase } from '../lib/supabase';

type Task = { id: string; title: string; status: 'todo' | 'doing' | 'done'; assignee: string | null };

export function TaskCard({ task, currentUser }: { task: Task; currentUser: string }) {
  const [error, setError] = useState('');

  async function advance() {
    const next = task.status === 'todo' ? 'doing' : task.status === 'doing' ? 'done' : null;
    if (!next) return setError('Task already done');
    if (next === 'doing' && task.assignee && task.assignee !== currentUser) {
      return setError('Only the assignee can start this task');
    }
    const { count } = await supabase.from('tasks').select('*', { count: 'exact', head: true })
      .eq('assignee', currentUser).eq('status', 'doing');
    if (next === 'doing' && (count ?? 0) >= 3) return setError('Finish a task before starting another');
    await supabase.from('tasks').update({ status: next, assignee: task.assignee ?? currentUser }).eq('id', task.id);
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <h3>{task.title}</h3>
      <button onClick={advance}>Advance</button>
      {error && <p role="alert">{error}</p>}
    </div>
  );
}

```

### Architectural Flaws in Original Code
1. **Workflow rules in a click handler:** status order, assignee ownership and the work-in-progress limit of 3 live in a React component.
2. **Database client in the UI:** Supabase queries run directly from the view.
3. **Inconsistent check order:** the WIP query runs even when the task is moving to `done`.

---

### Clean Architecture Refactoring

#### 1. Domain Layer (`src/domain/task.ts`)
```typescript
import { Result } from '../shared/Result';

export type TaskStatus = 'todo' | 'doing' | 'done';

export interface Task {
  readonly id: string;
  readonly title: string;
  readonly status: TaskStatus;
  readonly assignee: string | null;
}

export type AdvanceError =
  | { kind: 'AlreadyDone' }
  | { kind: 'NotAssignee' }
  | { kind: 'WipLimitReached'; limit: number };

export const WIP_LIMIT = 3;

export function advance(task: Task, user: string, userTasksInProgress: number): Result<Task, AdvanceError> {
  if (task.status === 'done') return Result.fail({ kind: 'AlreadyDone' });
  if (task.status === 'todo') {
    if (task.assignee && task.assignee !== user) return Result.fail({ kind: 'NotAssignee' });
    if (userTasksInProgress >= WIP_LIMIT) return Result.fail({ kind: 'WipLimitReached', limit: WIP_LIMIT });
    return Result.ok({ ...task, status: 'doing', assignee: task.assignee ?? user });
  }
  return Result.ok({ ...task, status: 'done', assignee: task.assignee ?? user });
}
```

#### 2. Application Layer (`src/application/AdvanceTask.ts`)
```typescript
import { Result } from '../shared/Result';
import { AdvanceError, Task, advance } from '../domain/task';

export interface TaskStore {
  countInProgress(user: string): Promise<number>;
  update(task: Task): Promise<void>;
}

export class AdvanceTask {
  constructor(private readonly tasks: TaskStore) {}

  async execute(task: Task, user: string): Promise<Result<Task, AdvanceError>> {
    const inProgress = task.status === 'todo' ? await this.tasks.countInProgress(user) : 0;
    const next = advance(task, user, inProgress);
    if (!next.success) return next;
    await this.tasks.update(next.data);
    return next;
  }
}
```

#### 3. Infrastructure Layer (`src/infrastructure/SupabaseTaskStore.ts`)
```typescript
import type { SupabaseClient } from '@supabase/supabase-js';
import { Task } from '../domain/task';
import { TaskStore } from '../application/AdvanceTask';

export class SupabaseTaskStore implements TaskStore {
  constructor(private readonly client: SupabaseClient) {}

  async countInProgress(user: string): Promise<number> {
    const { count } = await this.client.from('tasks').select('*', { count: 'exact', head: true })
      .eq('assignee', user).eq('status', 'doing');
    return count ?? 0;
  }

  async update(task: Task): Promise<void> {
    await this.client.from('tasks').update({ status: task.status, assignee: task.assignee }).eq('id', task.id);
  }
}
```

#### 4. Presentation Layer (`src/ui/TaskCard.tsx`)
```tsx
const MESSAGES: { [K in AdvanceError['kind']]: string } = {
  AlreadyDone: 'Task already done',
  NotAssignee: 'Only the assignee can start this task',
  WipLimitReached: 'Finish a task before starting another',
};

export function TaskCard({ task, currentUser, advanceTask }: { task: Task; currentUser: string; advanceTask: AdvanceTask }) {
  const [error, setError] = useState('');

  async function onAdvance() {
    const result = await advanceTask.execute(task, currentUser);
    setError(result.success ? '' : MESSAGES[result.error.kind]);
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <h3>{task.title}</h3>
      <button onClick={onAdvance}>Advance</button>
      {error && <p role="alert">{error}</p>}
    </div>
